g_cdc: NCM instead of ECM for the network half; f_ncm's timer at 50 us.

The iPad's link to the Mac is g_cdc's network function, and the root
filesystem is NFS over it.  macOS drives a CDC ECM interface with a
user-space DriverKit driver (AppleUserECM, which outranks its ECM kext),
one USB transfer per Ethernet frame; for CDC NCM (subclass 13) it has a
kernel driver, com.apple.driver.usb.cdc.ncm, and NCM batches frames into
each transfer -- an eighth of the interrupts on the iPad.  The gadget stays
the one legacy composite (bound from its own initcall, its MACs and qmult
from the same module parameters, usb0 and ttyGS0 as before); only ECM's
function becomes NCM's.

It is not what made the link faster: NCM alone read 10 MB/s instead of 9.
That was the controller's DMA bursts (0001, dwc2_set_s5l8940x_params:
27 MB/s).  And f_ncm holds a frame until its NTB is full or a 300 us timer
runs out; over NFS most frames from the iPad go alone, so every round trip
paid it: a cold metadata walk (find /usr/share) took 4.5 s instead of
ECM's 3.5.  At 50 us it takes 3.0.

